["Here's a basic example of how you might design your random number generator service in C++. Note that this is a simplified version and doesn't include all the features you requested (like caching, integration with logging systems, etc.), but it should give you a good starting point:

```cpp
#include <random>
#include <mutex>

class RandomNumberGenerator {
public:
    RandomNumberGenerator() : device(), engine(device()) {}

    int nextInt(int minInclusive, int maxExclusive) {
        std::uniform_int_distribution<int> distribution(minInclusive, maxExclusive - 1);
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        return distribution(engine);
    }

    double nextDouble(double minInclusive, double maxExclusive) {
        std::uniform_real_distribution<double> distribution(minInclusive, maxExclusive);
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        return distribution(engine);
    }

    bool nextBoolean() {
        return nextInt(0, 2) == 1;
    }

private:
    std::random_device device;
    std::mt19937 engine; // Mersenne Twister is a popular PRNG algorithm
    std::mutex mutex;
};
```

In this code, each method locks the mutex before generating a random number. This prevents concurrent threads from interfering with each other's random engines and generating identical sequences. The constructor initializes the RNG engine using `std::random_device` as a seed, which provides good entropy for PRNGs.

In a real-world scenario you would want to add logging, error handling, configuration management tools integration, caching mechanism etc. This is just a basic example of how it might be done in C++.
