Here's the Rust function you requested:

Please note that Rust has a different way of dealing with environment variables, so we need to make use of the `std::env` module here. We will also utilize the `std::path::Path` for creating directory paths and handling errors.

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::{self, DirEntry};
use std::io::ErrorKind;
use std::sync::Mutex;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;

struct ProjectDirectory {
    directory: Mutex<Option<DirEntry>>,
}

impl ProjectDirectory {
    fn new(path: &str) -> Self {
        let dir = match fs::read_dir(path) {
            Ok(mut entries) => Some(entries.next().unwrap()), // Assuming a single directory in the path
            Err(ref e) if e.kind() == ErrorKind::NotFound => None,
            Err(e) => panic_anyhow_error("Error creating project directory: {}", e),
        };

        Self {
            directory: Mutex::new(dir),
        }
    }

    fn get_or_create(&self, path: &str) -> Option<DirEntry> {
        match self.directory.lock().unwrap() {
            Some(ref entry) => Some(*entry), // Return existing directory
            None => Self::new(path).directory.lock().take(), // Create new and return it
        }
    }
}

fn initialize_project(dir: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let project_dir_env = env::var("PROJECT_DIR_PATH").unwrap_or(dir.into());

    // Check if directory already exists
    match fs::metadata(&project_dir_env) {
        Ok(_) => (), // Directory exists, do nothing
        Err(ref e) if e.kind() == ErrorKind::NotFound => 
            match fs::DirBuilder::new().current_dir().create(&project_dir_env)? {
                _ => env::set_var("PROJECT_DIR_PATH", &project_dir_env), // Set the environment variable if a new dir was created
            },
        Err(e) => return Err(e.into()), // Error creating directory, propagate error upwards
    }

    Ok(())
}
```
Please note that the Rust code is more complex than C# due to Rust's ownership and borrowing system, which prevents multiple threads from accessing or modifying data at the same time.  This also allows us to handle errors in a way that integrates well with Rust's error handling mechanism. The `ProjectDirectory` struct helps manage this by ensuring only one thread can access or create the project directory at any given time, and the rest will wait until they get their turn.
']

['```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.lang.StringBuilder;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.*;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

@Service
public class StringHasher {
    private static final Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger(StringHasher.class);
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @Async("asyncExecutor")
    public void processStrings(List<String> strings) {
        for (String string : strings) {
            try {
                if (!cache.containsKey(string)) {
                    String hashValue = hashFunction(string, "MD5");
                    cache.putIfAbsent(string, hashValue);
                }
            } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
                logger.error("Error hashing string", e);
            }
        }
    }

    public String getCachedHash(String input) {
        return cache.get(input);
    }

    private String hashFunction(String input, String algorithm) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance(algorithm);
        byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : hashBytes) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
``` 

This is a basic implementation of the service you requested. However, it's important to note that there are many other factors and trade-offs that would need to be made based on your specific application and infrastructure needs. This is just a starting point, and can be extended as needed for the additional requirements you mentioned (thread-safety with ConcurrentHashMap, efficient memory usage, error handling, extensibility for different hash algorithms, integration into existing data processing pipeline, and so forth). For example, more sophisticated caching strategies could be used to handle larger datasets or outliers in string lengths.
']

