['Below is a basic implementation of this functionality in Java using ConcurrentHashMap to achieve thread-safe operations, while minimizing redundant processing and improving performance with caching session identifiers. Also, it logs detailed metrics and performance statistics using SLF4J Logger. 

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.slf4j.*;

public class SessionProcessor {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SessionProcessor.class);

    // Cache to store session identifiers
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, Instant> cache;

    public SessionProcessor() {
        this.cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    }

    public int processLogFile(File logFile) throws IOException {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(logFile))) {
            String line;

            // Map to store session data for this batch of events
            ConcurrentMap<String, Instant> sessionsInBatch = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                String[] eventData = line.split("\\s+"); // Assume each line contains a timestamp followed by an event type

                if (eventData.length < 2) {
                    logger.error("Corrupted data: {}", line);
                    continue;
                }

                Instant timestamp = Instant.parse(eventData[0]); // Assume timestamps are ISO-8601 strings
                String sessionId = eventData[1]; // Session IDs are expected to be the first field in each line

                sessionsInBatch.putIfAbsent(sessionId, timestamp);
            }

            cache.putAll(sessionsInBatch);
        } catch (IOException | DateTimeParseException e) {
            logger.error("Error processing log file", e);
        }

        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Log session count and processing time
        logger.info("Processed {} sessions in {} ms", sessionsInBatch.size(), (endTime - startTime));

        return cache.size();
    }
}
```
This code assumes that each line in the log file contains a timestamp followed by an event type, separated by whitespace. The first field is assumed to be the session ID. 

Please replace `logger.error("Corrupted data: {}", line);` and `Instant.parse(eventData[0]);` with appropriate error handling code for corrupted data or invalid timestamps respectively.

It also uses SLF4J Logger to log the total number of unique sessions identified, processing time and any errors that occur during execution. You need to add logging framework dependency in your project's build file (like Maven pom.xml or Gradle build.gradle) to use this feature. For example, using Maven:
