['Here is a simple implementation of a connection manager using Java's ExecutorService and Future objects to handle concurrent requests efficiently. The ConnectionPool class uses a BlockingQueue to manage available connections, automatically scaling up or down based on load. It also uses the Future interface for handling failed connections without blocking other threads.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ConnectionManager {
    private final ExecutorService executor;
    private final BlockingQueue<Future<Connection>> queue;
    private volatile boolean shutdown = false;

    public ConnectionManager(int maxConnections) {
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(maxConnections);
        this.queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(maxConnections, true);
    }

    public Future<Connection> getConnection() {
        if (shutdown) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Connection manager is shut down");
        }

        // Get a connection from the queue, or create a new one if none are available.
        Future<Connection> future = queue.poll();
        if (future == null) {
            future = executor.submit(this::createConnection);
        }

        return future;
    }

    private Connection createConnection() {
        // Create a new connection to a remote service.
        return new RemoteService().getConnection();
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        this.shutdown = true;
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

This is just a basic implementation and doesn't cover all the requirements such as handling transient failures, dynamic scaling and connection pooling. Implementing these would require additional complexity and is beyond the scope of this simple example. You might also need to add more sophisticated logic for load balancing, reconnection strategies and so on depending upon your specific needs.
']

