['Sure, here's a basic implementation in C using `fork`, `execve`, and `waitpid`. Please note that this is just a basic skeleton and does not include all the features you requested but it should give you a starting point. You might need to expand it according to your needs:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <time.h>

#define MAX_COMMANDS 5
#define TIMEOUT 10

void handleAlarm(int signal) {
    // Do nothing, just wake up alarm()
}

int executeCommands(char* commands) {
    char *command = strtok(commands, ";");
    int i = 0;
    int status = 1;

    while (command != NULL && i < MAX_COMMANDS) {
        pid_t pid = fork();

        if (pid == 0) { // Child process
            char *args[] = {"sh", "-c", command, NULL};

            alarm(TIMEOUT);
            signal(SIGALRM, handleAlarm);

            execve("/bin/sh", args, NULL);

            return 0; // If execve() fails, terminate the child process.
        } else if (pid < 0) { // Fork failed
            perror("fork");
            status = 0;
            break;
        } else { // Parent process
            signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_IGN); // Avoid zombie processes

            int childStatus;
            waitpid(pid, &childStatus, 0);

            if (WIFEXITED(childStatus) && WEXITSTATUS(childStatus) != 0) {
                status = 0;
            }
        }

        command = strtok(NULL, ";"); // Next command
        i++;
    }

    while (waitpid(-1, NULL, 0) > 0); // Reap all child processes

    return status;
}
```
This function will handle the given set of commands in a loop until it reaches the maximum number of concurrent background processes or runs out of commands. It sets an alarm for each command execution to prevent them from running too long, and ignores SIGCHLD signals to avoid creating zombie processes. After executing all the commands, it waits for all child processes to exit.

Please note that this implementation does not include any error handling or cleanup code to handle external data source failures, such as network timeouts or file access errors, which would be necessary in a real-world application. Also, the code assumes that `sh` is available on the system and can execute arbitrary commands, which might not always be the case. Finally, this implementation does not handle concurrency between different calls to `executeCommands()`, so it's not safe for use in multi-threaded programs without additional synchronization.
