['Below is an example code snippet in C# which does some or all of what you have asked for. Please be aware that creating a complete solution as specified would require more than just the provided code and might involve additional considerations such as security, networking, multi-threading etc. This is merely a starting point:

```Csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Text.Json;
using kafka4net;

public class ConnectionManager
{
    private Producer _producer;
    public ConnectionManager()
    {
        // Initialize the Kafka producer.
        var options = new kafka4net.ProducerConfiguration
        {
            Topic = "connectionStatus",
            ServerUriList = new[] { "localhost:9092" },
        };
        _producer = new Producer(options);
    }

    public async Task ConnectAsync(string remoteAddress)
    {
        var retryDelay = 100; // initial delay in ms.
        const int maxRetries = 5; // max number of retries.

        for (var i = 0; i < maxRetries; ++i)
        {
            try
            {
                using var client = new WebClient();
                await _producer.SendMessageAsync(JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { status="Connecting...", remoteAddress }));

                // Determine compression format and load file into memory.
                Stream dataStream;
                if (remoteAddress.EndsWith(".gz"))
                    dataStream = new GZipInputStream(await client.OpenReadTaskAsync(remoteAddress));
                else if (remoteAddress.EndsWith(".zip"))
                    dataStream = await client.OpenReadTaskAsync(remoteAddress); // ZIP format not supported in .NET 
                else
                    throw new InvalidOperationException("Unknown compression format");

                // Load file into memory efficiently.
                var data = new byte[dataStream.Length];
                await dataStream.ReadAsync(data, 0, data.Length);

                await _producer.SendMessageAsync(JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { status="Connected", remoteAddress }));

                return; // Successfully connected and loaded file into memory.
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Connection failed: " + ex);
                await _producer.SendMessageAsync(JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { status="Failed", remoteAddress, error=ex.ToString() }));

                // Wait before retrying.
                await Task.Delay(retryDelay);
                retryDelay *= 2; // Exponential backoff.
            }
        }

        throw new Exception("Unable to connect after " + maxRetries + " attempts");
    }
}
```
In this snippet, the application uses Apache Kafka's Producer API to send connection status updates to a Kafka topic. This is just one way of handling such notifications and can be adapted according to your system requirements. The file loading part is kept simple for demonstration purposes. Real-world implementations would involve more complex error handling, possibly in the form of automatic recovery or fallback mechanisms, depending on the nature of the data being loaded.
']

