['Here is a simple implementation, considering you provided no specific requirements or constraints. This example only covers some aspects like thread safety using semaphores for synchronization, random number generation, logging mechanism, integration with an external data storage service and dynamic resizing of arrays. 

```cpp
#include <semaphore>
#include <mutex>
#include <random>
#include <fstream>
#include <ctime>
// Assume ExternalDataStorageService is a class provided by the external data storage service
#include "ExternalDataStorageService.hpp" 

class RecordManager {
private:
    std::vector<int> keys;
    std::vector<long> offsets;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::ofstream logFile;
    ExternalDataStorageService storageService;
    std::mt19937_64 mtRand;
public:
    RecordManager() : mtRand(std::random_device{}()) {
        keys.reserve(100);
        offsets.reserve(100);
        logFile.open("logfile.txt");
    }
    ~RecordManager() {
        logFile.close();
    }

    void generateRandomKeysAndOffsets() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        for (size_t i = 0; i < keys.capacity(); ++i) {
            keys[i] = mtRand();
            offsets[i] = mtRand();
        }
    }

    void logAccess(int key) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        time_t now = time(0);
        char* dt = ctime(&now);
        logFile << "Key: " << key << ", Timestamp: " << dt;
    }

    int getRecordCount() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        return keys.size();
    }

    void reorderRecords(bool (*comparator)(int, long)) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        // Use the comparator function to sort records
        std::sort(keys.begin(), keys.end(), [&](int a, int b) {
            return comparator(a, offsets[a]);
        });
    }

    void storeRecords() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        for (size_t i = 0; i < keys.size(); ++i) {
            storageService.storeRecord(keys[i], offsets[i]);
        }
    }
};
```
The RecordManager class provides the basic functionality you requested. It generates random keys and offsets, logs accesses to a file, gets the current record count, reorders records based on a custom comparator function provided by the user, and stores the records in an external data storage service. All functions are thread-safe using locks. The logFile is protected by the mutex_ for safe concurrent writes.

Please note that the above code is just to give you an idea of how such a system could be implemented in C++14. It doesn't handle all the features and requirements requested. You might need to adjust it based on your specific needs and constraints. This includes but not limited to, more detailed logging mechanism, dynamic resizing of keys and offsets arrays, dealing with unexpected data volume changes, better error handling etc.
