['Handling of edge cases, such as null pointers or memory allocation failures. This will allow your application to function smoothly even under resource constraints and unexpected conditions, providing a robust foundation for continued operation. Below is a basic outline of how you might implement these tasks in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

// Define the structure for geometry
typedef struct {
    float x, y, z;
    uint32_t index;
    char label[50];
} geometry;

// Initialize hash table size and create the hash table array
const int HASH_TABLE_SIZE = 1000;
geometry* hashTable[HASH_TABLE_SIZE] = { NULL };
int numElementsInHashTable = 0;

void memoryAllocationFailure() {
    printf("Memory allocation failure. Exiting program.\n");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

// Allocate and initialize arrays for coordinates, indices and labels
float* x = (float*)malloc(500 * sizeof(float));
if (!x) memoryAllocationFailure();
memset(x, 0, 500 * sizeof(float));
uint32_t* index = (uint32_t*)calloc(500, sizeof(uint32_t));
if (!index) memoryAllocationFailure();
char(*label)[50] = malloc(500 * 50);
if (!label) memoryAllocationFailure();
memset(label, 0, 500 * 50);

// Add data to hash table (collision resolution by separate chaining)
int addToHashTable(geometry* g) {
    int index = g->index % HASH_TABLE_SIZE;
    geometry* current = hashTable[index];
    while (current != NULL && current->index != g->index) {
        if (current->next == NULL) {
            current->next = malloc(sizeof(geometry));
            if (!current->next) memoryAllocationFailure();
        }
        current = current->next;
    }
    if (current != NULL) return 0; // Already exists in hash table

    g->next = hashTable[index];
    hashTable[index] = g;
    numElementsInHashTable++;

    // Rehash if necessary
    if ((float)numElementsInHashTable / HASH_TABLE_SIZE > 0.75) {
        rehash();
    }
}
```
Remember to add error handling for edge cases like null pointers and memory allocation failures. This is a basic outline and you would need to implement the full functionality of your system according to its specific requirements. It's also important to consider trade-offs between different strategies when making design decisions. The code above uses a simple hash table with separate chaining for collision resolution, which might not be suitable for all applications, but it should provide a good starting point for further optimization and testing.
