```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>

// Define the struct for DeviceLog 
typedef struct {
    int deviceID;
    time_t timestamp;
    char logLevel[20]; // assuming we have a finite number of possible log levels and they are always less than 20 chars long
    char* message;
} DeviceLog;

// Define the linked list node for storing logs
typedef struct Node {
    DeviceLog data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

// Initialize the head of the linked list
Node *head = NULL;

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; // for synchronization

void addLog(int deviceID, char* logLevel, char* message) {
    if (strlen(logLevel) == 0 || strlen(message) == 0){
        return;
    }

    Node *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node)); // allocate memory for new node
    newNode->data.deviceID = deviceID;
    newNode->data.timestamp = time(NULL);
    strncpy(newNode->data.logLevel, logLevel, sizeof(newNode->data.logLevel) - 1); // copy log level into node
    newNode->data.message = malloc(strlen(message) + 1); // allocate memory for message
    strcpy(newNode->data.message, message); // copy message into node

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); // lock the mutex before modifying shared data

    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // unlock the mutex after modification is done
}

// Similarly implement functions for filtering, removing old logs, handling network failures etc. 
```

The above C code provides a basic structure of linked list where each node contains a `DeviceLog` struct and a pointer to the next node in the list. The function `addLog()` takes deviceID, logLevel and message as input and creates a new Node storing this data at the head of the list. This operation is guarded by a mutex, ensuring that only one thread can modify the linked list at any given time, avoiding race conditions.

To handle scalability, you might want to consider using multiple threads for different tasks such as adding logs, filtering, removing old logs etc., or use a concurrent data structure like Java's `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` in C++. 

Performance of the synchronization mechanism heavily depends on the specific environment and system load, you may need to tweak it according to your needs and testing results. A common strategy is to avoid locking for as long as possible, which can be achieved by using a "reader/writer" lock where multiple threads can read shared data simultaneously but only one thread can write at any given time.

Memory management is also critical in distributed systems where resources are limited. One approach is to use pool-based memory allocation where a large block of memory is allocated and recycled, rather than allocating/deallocating memory for each log entry individually. This can reduce fragmentation and improve cache locality. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and in a real system, there would be more complexity and considerations like handling concurrent access across multiple instances of the application, network failures, retry logic, failover scenarios etc. It's recommended to have experienced software engineers handle these aspects as they involve complex trade-offs and need to be carefully designed for each specific scenario.
