```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <openssl/md5.h> // Make sure to install OpenSSL

void process_large_file(const char* filename, int chunk_size) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "rb");
    if (!file) {
        printf("Error: Unable to open file %s\n", filename);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    MD5_CTX mdContext;
    MD5_Init(&mdContext);

    unsigned char data[chunk_size];
    size_t bytesRead;
    while ((bytesRead = fread(data, 1, chunk_size, file)) != 0) {
        MD5_Update(&mdContext, data, bytesRead);
    }

    unsigned char digest[MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    MD5_Final(digest, &mdContext);

    // Convert the hash to hexadecimal string for presentation.
    char mdString[33];
    for (int i = 0; i < MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++) {
        sprintf(&mdString[i*2], "%02x", (unsigned int)digest[i]);
    }

    printf("MD5 hash: %s\n", mdString);

    fclose(file);
}
```
This is a basic implementation for processing large files and generating MD5 hashes. The code reads the file in chunks (of size `chunk_size`), updates the MD5 context with each chunk, and finally produces the final hash. 

Please note that error handling, resuming from specific offsets or dealing with different file types is not included due to the complexity of these features and limitations on block sizes in this platform. This would require a much more comprehensive answer than can be provided here. It would also involve more advanced concepts like memory management strategies, multi-threading for parallel processing etc., which would make the implementation significantly larger and complex.
