    const char* query = "INSERT INTO profiles (userID, profile) VALUES (?, ?);";

    sqlite3_stmt *stmt;
    if(sqlite3_prepare_v2(db, query, -1, &stmt, NULL)) {
        return -1; // Handle error
    }

    if(sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, profile->name, -1, SQLITE_STATIC) != SQLITE_OK ||
       sqlite3_bind_blob(stmt, 2, encryptedProfile, encryptedLength, SQLITE_STATIC) != SQLITE_OK) {
        return -1; // Handle error
    }

    if(sqlite3_step(stmt) != SQLITE_DONE) {
        return -1; // Handle error
    }

    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(ctx);
    free(encryptedProfile);
    sqlite3_finalize(stmt);

    return 0; // Success
}
```
This is just a simple example of how to start implementing this, and it does not include the error handling, logging mechanism for database connection errors or conflicts, caching layer implementation, schema changes management, and optimization considerations. It also assumes that UserProfile structure can be directly serialized to JSON, which might not be true in real-world applications. For a full-fledged application, you would need to use more advanced techniques such as distributed database writes using sharding or partitioning, caching with consideration for consistency and latency requirements, and proper error handling and recovery from transient failures.
