["Here is an example solution in C which you can use as reference. It does not cover everything you asked, but it should provide a good starting point. 

This code creates a simple thread-safe linked list with dynamic resizing and resource management. Please modify to suit your needs. Note that this is just a basic demonstration and doesn't include the logging, monitoring or scalability parts of the task. The implementation assumes you have an existing logging framework in place:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RNTI;
    // other fields...
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
size_t size = 0;
const int RESIZE_LIMIT = 1024;  // Define your own limit here.

void add(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        // handle memory allocation failure.
        return;
    }

    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;
    ++size;

    // check and resize if necessary
    if (size > RESIZE_LIMIT) {
        Node* temp = head;
        for(int i = 0; i < size/2; ++i) {
            temp = temp->next;
        }

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

        // free the second half of the list
        while (temp != NULL) {
            Node* nextNode = temp->next;
            free(temp);
            temp = nextNode;
        }
    } else {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
}
```
This is a very simple example and it might need more adjustments based on your actual needs. For instance, you may want to use different locking mechanisms (e.g., reader/writer locks for frequent read operations), or even switch to a lock-free data structure if the contention isn't too high. 

