['Here's a basic outline of how you could implement this in C using POSIX threads (pthread) and a custom thread-safe linked list implementation for storing results from worker threads. Please note that this is a simplified example, and actual production code would need more careful design considerations and error handling.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h> // For usleep function to simulate rate limits
#include <time.h> 

// Define a node in our thread-safe linked list
typedef struct Node {
    double data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

// Linked list with head and tail, along with mutex for locking operations
Node *head = NULL;
Node *tail = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t headLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_mutex_t tailLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Add data to our thread-safe linked list
void addToList(double data) {
    Node *current = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    current->data = data;
    current->next = NULL;

    // Get lock on tail, and append new node
    pthread_mutex_lock(&tailLock); 
    if (head == NULL) {
        head = current;
    } else {
        tail->next = current;
    }
    tail = current;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&tailLock); // Release lock on tail
}

// Worker thread function, simulating network latency and processing data
void *worker(void* args) {
    double startTime = clock();

    // Simulate network latency (50-100ms)
    usleep((rand() % 51 + 50) * 1000);

    // Simulate error (1% of tasks fail)
    if(rand() % 100 < 1) {
        printf("Task failed\n");
    } else {
        double endTime = clock();
        addToList((endTime - startTime) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC); // Add processing time to list
    }

    return NULL;
}

// Main function for managing concurrent tasks
double manageTasks(int numTasks, int numThreads) {
    pthread_t threads[numThreads];
    double totalTime = 0.0;
    int completedTasks = 0;

    // Spawn worker threads and join them all
    for (int i = 0; i < numTasks; i += numThreads) {
        for(int j = 0; j < numThreads && i + j < numTasks; ++j){
            pthread_create(&threads[j], NULL, worker, NULL);
        }

        // Wait for all threads to finish and collect their results
        for (int j = 0; j < numThreads && i + j < numTasks; ++j) {
            pthread_join(threads[j], NULL);
            ++completedTasks;
        }
    }

    // Calculate average processing time
    Node *node = head;
    while (node != NULL) {
        totalTime += node->data;
        Node* temp = node;
        node = node->next;
        free(temp); // Free memory to avoid leaks
    }

    return completedTasks ? totalTime / completedTasks : 0.0;
}
```
This code does not handle rate limits imposed by the API, it simply simulates them with `usleep` call in `worker` function (which causes a delay proportional to task processing time). Implementation of handling rate limits will depend on how that's defined for your specific use case. 

Please note this is a simple implementation and may need adjustments for production code, such as error checking, logging, memory management, synchronization primitives etc. This also assumes the external API can be simulated accurately with `usleep` calls which might not always be accurate. In real-world use case, you'd likely use specific libraries or tools provided by the API service provider to handle rate limits and network requests.
