['Creating a dynamic list with thread safety, error logging and handling memory allocation failures in C is complex task. Below is a simplified example on how to implement such a functionality considering some trade-offs:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct UEContext {
    int cellID;
    int UEID;
    int PDUSessionID;
} UEContext;

typedef struct Node {
    UEContext* context;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL; 
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addContext(int cellID, int UEID, int PDUSessionID) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock mutex for thread safety.

    Node *node = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if(node == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed at %ld\n", time(NULL));
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock mutex before returning in case of error.
        return;
    }

    UEContext* context = (UEContext*) malloc(sizeof(UEContext));
    if(context == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed at %ld\n", time(NULL));
        free(node); // Free allocated memory in case of error.
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); 
        return;
    }

    context->cellID = cellID;
    context->UEID = UEID;
    context->PDUSessionID = PDUSessionID;

    node->context = context;
    node->next = head;
    head = node; // Add new node to the head of the list.

    printf("Context added successfully at %ld\n", time(NULL));

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock mutex after successful operation.
}
```
This code provides a basic structure for handling dynamic list with thread safety and error logging, however it's simplistic and doesn't take into account all the mentioned complexities. For example, there is no function to remove contexts from the list, no efficient scaling mechanism, no cache optimization and so on. A fully functional version would be much more complex and involve a significant amount of additional code. 

