['Here's a basic implementation of what you are asking for. It uses a linked list with locks to prevent concurrent access from different threads. The cleanup function will periodically scan through all records, deleting any that have been unused for some time. 

Please note that this is just an example and may not be optimal in all cases as it heavily depends on the specific use case and requirements. You might want to adjust it according to your needs. Also note that testing and benchmarking are required for optimizing cleanup frequency and linked list structure.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>

typedef struct record {
    int id;  // example field
    struct record* next;
} Record;

Record* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
time_t lastCleanup = 0;
const time_t CLEANUP_INTERVAL = 10;  // adjust as needed

void cleanup() {
    time_t now = time(NULL);

    if (now - lastCleanup >= CLEANUP_INTERVAL) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

        Record* current = head;
        Record* previous = NULL;

        while (current != NULL) {
            // replace this with a check for whether the record is unused or not
            if (current->id % 2 == 0) {  
                if (previous == NULL) {
                    head = current->next;
                } else {
                    previous->next = current->next;
                }

                free(current);

                if (previous == NULL) {
                    current = head;
                } else {
                    current = previous->next;
                }
            } else {
                previous = current;
                current = current->next;
            }
        }

        lastCleanup = now;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
}

void insertRecord(int id) {
    Record* newRecord = malloc(sizeof(Record));

    if (newRecord == NULL) {
        return;  // handle error appropriately in your code
    }

    newRecord->id = id;
    newRecord->next = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (head == NULL) {
        head = newRecord;
    } else {
        Record* current = head;

        while (current->next != NULL) {
            current = current->next;
        }

        current->next = newRecord;
    }

    cleanup();  // perform periodic cleanup
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

Please note that the "unused" check in the `cleanup` function is just an example and should be replaced with a mechanism to determine if a record has been unused. Also, for real-world applications you'd want to use more efficient data structures such as a hash table for insertion/deletion operations and a separate garbage collection thread for cleaning up unused records.
