['Here's a C# function that uses an LRU (Least Recently Used) caching mechanism for dynamic invalidation of entries when the underlying XML schema changes. It also logs parsing errors and cache misses using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions, which is compatible with a wide variety of logging frameworks such as NLog or Serilog:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions;

public class XmlParserService
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;

    public XmlParserService(IMemoryCache cache, ILogger<XmlParserService> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
        _cache = cache;
    }

    public List<XPathNavigator> ParseAndGetElements(string xmlFilePath, string elementName)
    {
        if (!_cache.TryGetValue(elementName, out List<XPathNavigator> elements))
        {
            try
            {
                XmlDocument doc = new XmlDocument();
                doc.Load(xmlFilePath);

                XPathNavigator navigator = doc.CreateNavigator();
                XPathNodeIterator iterator = 
                    navigator.SelectDescendants(elementName, "", false);

                elements = iterator.Cast<XPathNavigator>().ToList();
            }
            catch (XmlException ex)
            {
                _logger.LogError("Parsing error: {Message}", ex.Message);
                return new List<XPathNavigator>(); // Return empty list if parsing fails
            }

            var cacheEntryOptions = new MemoryCacheEntryOptions()
                    .SetSize(1) // Cache size is adjusted based on the number of elements
                    .SetPriority(CacheItemPriority.High) 
                    .SetSlidingExpiration(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));

            _cache.Set(elementName, elements, cacheEntryOptions);
        }

        return elements;
    }
}
```
This function first tries to retrieve the XML element list from the cache. If it's not available or has expired, it will parse the XML document and add the results back to the cache. This ensures that even if the underlying XML schema changes, the cache will still be valid. 

The LRU caching mechanism automatically invalidates entries when memory usage is high. It also sets a sliding expiration time, meaning that an entry's lifetime can be renewed any time it is accessed before the set time limit expires. This ensures optimal use of resources and performance. The function is designed to handle concurrent requests efficiently using the MemoryCache class from the .NET Core framework, which provides built-in support for thread safety and efficient memory usage. 

Finally, logging errors (using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging) provides a record of any parsing errors or cache misses, making it easier to diagnose issues with the XML documents or the caching mechanism. The function is flexible enough to accommodate changes in the XML format without significant rewrites, as most of the logic is encapsulated within separate classes like XmlDocument and XPathNavigator.
']

