["Sure, here is an example of such a PHP function that uses MySQL transactions for data consistency and PDO prepared statements to prevent SQL injection. Note that this code assumes you have already set up your database connection using PDO:

```php
function updateUserInfo($userId, $userProfileData, $userPreferencesData, $subscriptionDetails) {
    $db = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=test', $user, $pass);

    // Begin a transaction to ensure data consistency across multiple tables
    $db->beginTransaction();

    try {
        // Prepare and execute SQL queries for each table
        $profileStmt = $db->prepare('UPDATE user_profiles SET profile = :profile WHERE userId = :userId');
        $preferencesStmt = $db->prepare('UPDATE user_preferences SET preferences = :preferences WHERE userId = :userId');
        $subscriptionStmt = $db->prepare('UPDATE subscriptions SET details = :details WHERE userId = :userId');

        // Bind values to the prepared statements and execute them
        $profileStmt->execute([':profile' => $userProfileData, ':userId' => $userId]);
        $preferencesStmt->execute([':preferences' => $userPreferencesData, ':userId' => $userId]);
        $subscriptionStmt->execute([':details' => $subscriptionDetails, ':userId' => $userId]);

        // Commit the transaction after successful execution of all queries
        $db->commit();
    } catch (PDOException $e) {
        // An error occurred; rollback the transaction and log the error message
        $db->rollBack();
        error_log('An error occurred: ' . $e->getMessage());

        return 'Failed to update user information. Error logged.';
    }

    return 'Successfully updated user information.';
}
```

This function first begins a transaction on the database connection, then updates all related tables in one go. If any error occurs during this process, it rolls back the transaction and logs the error message before returning a failure message. Otherwise, it commits the transaction to save changes and return a success message. 

This code is not meant for production use without further adjustments. It's advisable to add more robust checks for data types, missing fields or unexpected values in real-world applications. Furthermore, adding appropriate error reporting, logging mechanism, and handling race conditions would be highly recommended based on specific business logic requirements.
"]

