["Here is a basic implementation for managing sessions with C. This example uses POSIX threads (pthreads) for concurrency control and assumes that `DataTransferObject` structures have been defined elsewhere, and that memory handling and serialization to/from the external data source are handled by the application layer.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct DataTransferObject {
    int session_id;
    time_t timestamp; 
    void* payload;
    size_t payload_size;
} DataTransferObject;

DataTransferObject* sessionTable = NULL; // Session data array.
int tableSize = 0; // Current number of sessions in the array.
pthread_mutex_t lock;  // Mutex to control access to the session table.

// Add a new entry.
void addEntry(DataTransferObject* dto) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < tableSize; ++i) {
        if (sessionTable[i].session_id == dto->session_id) {
            // Duplicate session IDs are not allowed.
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return;
        }
    }

    tableSize++;
    sessionTable = realloc(sessionTable, sizeof(DataTransferObject)*tableSize); 
    memcpy(&sessionTable[tableSize-1], dto, sizeof(DataTransferObject)); // Deep copy payload if necessary.

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Update an existing entry.
void updateEntry(int session_id, DataTransferObject* dto) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < tableSize; ++i) {
        if (sessionTable[i].session_id == session_id) {
            memcpy(&sessionTable[i], dto, sizeof(DataTransferObject)); // Deep copy payload if necessary.
            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Retrieve an entry by session ID.
DataTransferObject* getEntry(int session_id) {
    DataTransferObject* result = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < tableSize; ++i) {
        if (sessionTable[i].session_id == session_id) {
            result = malloc(sizeof(DataTransferObject)); // Allocate memory for the DTO.
            memcpy(result, &sessionTable[i], sizeof(DataTransferObject)); // Deep copy payload if necessary.
            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return result;
}
```
This code uses a dynamically allocated array to store sessions, and pthreads for concurrency control. The array is reallocated whenever an entry is added or removed, which could be inefficient if the number of sessions changes frequently. A more efficient approach would use a data structure that supports dynamic resizing (such as a linked list) and handle memory fragmentation by periodically defragmenting the array.

Memory allocation error handling has been omitted for brevity, but could be implemented using `setrlimit` to set an upper limit on the total heap size or by checking return values from malloc/realloc calls. 

