#include "logging_system.h" // assuming you have an existing logging system

// Define the structure
typedef struct {
    int RNTI;
    int CRCFLAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Function to allocate memory for list of structures and populate them with random values ensuring no two consecutive structures have same CRCFLAG value. 
NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* allocateAndPopulate(size_t n, int seed) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock the mutex to prevent race condition

    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* list = malloc(n * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
    if (list == NULL) {
        logError("Failed to allocate memory for the list of structures.");
        return NULL; // Handle error gracefully
    }

    srand(seed); // Seed the random number generator. Defaults to system-generated value if seed is not provided
    int lastCRCFLAG = -1; // Initialize with an invalid value

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int newCRCFLAG;
        do {
            newCRCFLAG = rand(); // Generate a random number
        } while (newCRCFLAG == lastCRCFLAG); // Ensure no two consecutive structures have the same CRCFLAG value

        list[i].RNTI = i;
        list[i].CRCFLAG = newCRCFLAG;

        lastCRCFLAG = newCRCFLAG;
    }

    logInfo("Successfully allocated and populated the list of structures.");

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock the mutex after operation is done

    return list;
}
``` 
In this code, `allocateAndPopulate` function is thread-safe as it locks a mutex before starting the operation and unlocks it afterwards. This ensures that only one thread can allocate memory at a time to prevent race conditions.

The function also logs information about its operations, including successful allocation and validation of memory. The logging system is assumed to be an external component, so you would need to replace `logInfo` and `logError` with calls to your own logging functions. 

Remember this example does not handle errors that could occur during the periodical reclamation of memory or when the allocated memory exceeds the predefined maximum limit. Handling these scenarios in a multithreading environment is more complex and requires further considerations about synchronization, error handling etc.
']

